Q: How to suppress phantom errors in match operator using DataWeave 2.x A: <p>Instead of using <code>i</code> use more a more constrained variable in the case. That will help DataWeave to know the right type and the errors disappear:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>fun formatAnything(i,fmt) =
    i match {
        case s is String -&gt; fmt(s)
        case a is Array -&gt; a map fmt($)
        case o is Object -&gt; o mapObject (v,k) -&gt; (k): fmt(v)
        else -&gt; i
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Output from Playground:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;formatArrayOfString&quot;: [
    &quot;MANY PARTS OF THE PINE TREE ARE EDIBLE. &quot;,
    &quot;BUT THAT IS JUST CRAZY BECAUSE&quot;,
    &quot;WHO REALLY WANTS TO EAT A PINE TREE?&quot;
  ],
  &quot;formatObject&quot;: {
    &quot;airlineName&quot;: &quot;delta&quot;,
    &quot;availableSeats&quot;: &quot;40&quot;,
    &quot;departureDate&quot;: &quot;apr 11, 2018&quot;,
    &quot;destination&quot;: &quot;lax&quot;,
    &quot;flightCode&quot;: &quot;a134ds&quot;,
    &quot;price&quot;: &quot;750.00&quot;
  },
  &quot;formatString&quot;: &quot;many parts of the pine tree are edible. &quot;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Using <code>if</code> expressions clearly doesn't really help with the type, just with the condition for the case.</p>

Q: iOS Build Error: &#39;cannot find interface declaration for EXExpoAppDelegate&#39; after adding Expo to React Native 0.79 with Turbo Modules A: <p><strong>I managed to resolve this issue.</strong></p>
<p>The problem arises because when using <strong>React Native 0.79 (New Architecture)</strong> with <strong>Expo Modules</strong>, the generated Swift header for your project (<code>&lt;ProjectName&gt;-Swift.h</code>) references <code>ExpoAppDelegate</code> (via your <code>AppDelegate</code> class).</p>
<p>However, when you import this generated header into an Objective-C++ (<code>.mm</code>) TurboModule file, the C++ compiler does not automatically see the underlying Swift definitions from Expo, leading to the <code>cannot find interface declaration for 'EXExpoAppDelegate'</code> error.</p>
<p><strong>The Solution</strong></p>
<p>You need to explicitly import the Expo Swift headers inside your TurboModule's implementation file (<code>.mm</code>) <strong>before</strong> importing your project's generated Swift header.</p>
<p>Update your imports in <code>YourModule.mm</code> as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-obj-c++ prettyprint-override"><code>#import &quot;NativeModuleFoo.h&quot;

#import &quot;Expo-Swift.h&quot;
#import &quot;ExpoModulesCore-Swift.h&quot;

#import &quot;NewExp-Swift.h&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>By adding these imports, the compiler can correctly resolve the inheritance chain for <code>ExpoAppDelegate</code> and <code>ModulesProvider</code>.</p>
<p>Reference: This behavior was also discussed in a similar context on the Expo repository: <a href="https://github.com/expo/expo/issues/35388" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/expo/expo/issues/35388</a></p>

Q: Cannot skip repackage goal of spring-boot-maven-plugin A: <p>The simplest method is to exclude the goal entirely in child POM's, not limited to the &lt;execution&gt; block</p>
<p>e.g. in a child POM.xml</p>
<pre><code>    &lt;build&gt;
      &lt;plugins&gt;
        &lt;plugin&gt;
          &lt;groupId&gt;org.apache.maven.plugins&lt;/groupId&gt;
          &lt;artifactId&gt;maven-compiler-plugin&lt;/artifactId&gt;
        &lt;/plugin&gt;
        &lt;plugin&gt;
          &lt;groupId&gt;org.springframework.boot&lt;/groupId&gt;
          &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-boot-maven-plugin&lt;/artifactId&gt;
          &lt;configuration&gt;
              &lt;skip&gt;true&lt;/skip&gt;
          &lt;/configuration&gt;
        &lt;/plugin&gt;
      &lt;/plugins&gt;
    &lt;/build&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>where versions are supplied by dependency management in the parent POM.xml</p>
